module gunbc.host_realization

import std.types { String, Bool, Int, List }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalSubjectRef }

data host_realization_note: String = "CONTAINER IS NOT RENDERER IS NOT OPERATING SYSTEM IS NOT CAPABILITY PROVIDER (operator ruling, 2026-08-06). Four things get fused into the word 'platform' and every fusion costs a real distinction: a native desktop application can render the very same DOM surface a website renders; a website can render through DOM or through WebGPU; a native shell can host today's instrument unchanged while adding typed platform capability bindings underneath it; and the same operating system can appear under several containers at once. So this module carries the axes separately and NONE of them is the product's semantics — a finish line, a camera, a detent and a physical impulse mean the same thing in Chrome, in a Windows shell, on a phone, and in an engine scene.

WHAT THIS EXISTS TO PREVENT is a browser-shaped frontend architecture. The first cut of the motion observation carrier made BrowserProductRef, browsing context and secure context TOP-LEVEL fields of a supposedly neutral observation, which quietly declared that the product is a website and that every other container is a variant of one. It is the other way round: the web is one realization among several, grounded on its own terms, and it is not the preferred final container merely because it is the first one running."

data container_kind_note: String = "WHERE THE PRODUCT PROCESS LIVES. This axis is a closed structural enumeration and is deliberately NOT a product enumeration — Chrome, Safari, a signed desktop build and a phone app are symbolic references to their own authorities, carried beside this kind rather than inside it, so adding a product is a new module and edits nothing here (DESIGN §3). An embedded web view is its own arm rather than a browser document, because its permission surface and its policy delegation belong to the hosting application instead of to a browser.

COMPANION IS NOT A CONTAINER (review finding, 2026-08-06). An earlier revision listed CompanionDevice beside the four process locations, which fused two orthogonal questions: a companion phone still runs its process SOMEWHERE — a browser document, a native mobile process, or an embedded web view — and companion describes its role in the topology, not where the code lives. The role moved to HostRole below, so a phone acting as a deliberate physical controller for a desktop instrument is expressible as exactly what it is."

type ApplicationContainerKind
  = BrowserDocument
  | NativeDesktopProcess
  | NativeMobileProcess
  | EmbeddedWebView

fn container_kind_key(c: ApplicationContainerKind) -> String {
  match c {
    BrowserDocument => "browser-document"
    NativeDesktopProcess => "native-desktop-process"
    NativeMobileProcess => "native-mobile-process"
    EmbeddedWebView => "embedded-web-view"
  }
}

data host_role_note: String = "WHAT THIS HOST IS DOING IN THE TOPOLOGY, which is orthogonal to where its process lives. A phone can be the instrument itself or a deliberate physical controller for an instrument running elsewhere, and in both cases it is a native mobile process or a browser document. Keeping the role separate is what lets a companion input binding be modelled without inventing a container kind for it."

type HostRole
  = PrimaryInstrument
  | CompanionInput

fn host_role_key(r: HostRole) -> String {
  match r {
    PrimaryInstrument => "primary-instrument"
    CompanionInput => "companion-input"
  }
}

data process_lifetime_boolean_removed_note: String = "A PROCESS-LIFETIME BOOLEAN DERIVED FROM CONTAINER TYPE WAS WRONG TWICE OVER (review finding, 2026-08-06), and it is deleted rather than corrected. It claimed a native mobile application owns its process lifetime, which no mobile application does — the operating system suspends and kills it on its own policy. And it answered the wrong question: what the product actually needs is CONTINUITY of the instrument and its state, which is a capability with several independent parts, not a property assigned to a container by type. A native shell makes some of those parts easier to realize; it does not make them different requirements."

data continuity_polarity_note: String = "THE FOURTH FIELD WAS NAMED FOR THE FAILURE AND REQUIRED TO BE TRUE (review finding, 2026-08-06). The accepted law is that internal navigation PRESERVES the instrument and does not replay a cold start — three fields named the preservation and the fourth named the thing to avoid, and completeness then demanded all four be true, so the law read as 'preserve everything, and also throw it away'. A witness pinned false as incomplete, which made the inversion load-bearing rather than a slip someone would notice.

Renaming is the repair rather than flipping the required value, because a field whose name says one thing and whose required value says the opposite is a trap that reads correctly at every call site and is wrong. All four fields now name a property the instrument HAS, so completeness is the conjunction of four trues and no reader has to hold a negation in their head."

type InstrumentContinuity {
  preserve_local_camera: Bool
  preserve_last_projection: Bool
  preserve_preferences: Bool
  avoid_cold_start_replay: Bool
}

fn continuity_is_complete(c: InstrumentContinuity) -> Bool {
  c.preserve_local_camera
    && c.preserve_last_projection
    && c.preserve_preferences
    && c.avoid_cold_start_replay
}

data renderer_kind_note: String = "HOW A DECIDED PROJECTION BECOMES PIXELS AND ACCESSIBILITY STRUCTURE, and the product model must not know which one was chosen. This is a structural axis rather than a product one — there is no vendor here to enumerate — and it is orthogonal to the container above it: a native desktop process may render DOM inside a web view, and a browser document may render a WebGPU scene. Keeping the two apart is what leaves the route open from today's generated DOM instrument to a native or engine realization of the SAME projection, without touching roadmap semantics."

type RendererKind
  = DomCssRenderer
  | WebGpuRenderer
  | NativeWidgetRenderer
  | GameEngineRenderer

fn renderer_kind_key(r: RendererKind) -> String {
  match r {
    DomCssRenderer => "dom-css"
    WebGpuRenderer => "webgpu"
    NativeWidgetRenderer => "native-widget"
    GameEngineRenderer => "game-engine"
  }
}

data product_is_not_engine_note: String = "A BROWSER PRODUCT IS NOT ITS ENGINE, AND THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY RULED SO (review finding, 2026-08-06). extdeps.browser.chromium's own chromium_identity_boundary_note states that a Chromium build is not a Google Chrome, Opera, Edge, or Playwright identity. An earlier revision of this lane's fixture nevertheless recorded a Google Chrome observation with container pointing at the Chromium project and container_build carrying Chrome's version string — reopening exactly the product/engine conflation that ruling was written to close, and attributing a Chrome result to Chromium.

The two are separate fields. The product is what a person installed and what a receipt panel names; the runtime engine is what actually implements the contract and is frequently what a platform limitation belongs to. They resolve independently: a Chrome observation can name Chrome exactly while its Chromium revision stays unresolved, which is the common case under a reduced User-Agent."

data host_realization_identity_note: String = "Every identity here is a symbolic reference to the authority that owns it, never a name string, so a host is a join over real modules rather than over spellings (DESIGN §3 cite-the-symbol). The unresolved arm is a NAMED variant carrying its own cause rather than a bare optional, because there are several distinct reasons an identity goes unresolved and collapsing them into one absent value is the state-space conflation DESIGN's failure-mode list names first: a reduced User-Agent that reports a frozen literal, a probe that never asked, and a platform that publishes no such identity are three different situations with three different remedies, and only the first is fixed by reading UA Client Hints."

type ResolvedRef
  = RefResolved { declaration: DeclarationRef }
  | RefReducedByPolicy { reported: String }
  | RefNotProbed
  | RefUnpublishedByPlatform

fn ref_is_resolved(r: ResolvedRef) -> Bool {
  match r {
    RefResolved { declaration } => true
    RefReducedByPolicy { reported } => false
    RefNotProbed => false
    RefUnpublishedByPlatform => false
  }
}

fn resolved_ref_key(r: ResolvedRef) -> String {
  match r {
    RefResolved { declaration } => "resolved"
    RefReducedByPolicy { reported } => "reduced-by-policy"
    RefNotProbed => "not-probed"
    RefUnpublishedByPlatform => "unpublished-by-platform"
  }
}

type HostRealization {
  platform: ExternalSubjectRef
  platform_revision: ResolvedRef
  device_identity: ResolvedRef
  role: HostRole
  container_kind: ApplicationContainerKind
  container_product: DeclarationRef
  container_product_build: ResolvedRef
  runtime_engine: ResolvedRef
  runtime_engine_build: ResolvedRef
  renderer: RendererKind
}

fn host_identity_is_exact(h: HostRealization) -> Bool {
  ref_is_resolved(r: h.platform_revision) &&
    ref_is_resolved(r: h.device_identity) &&
    ref_is_resolved(r: h.container_product_build)
}

data host_identity_exactness_note: String = "A host whose revision, device and build are all resolved can carry a claim about THAT machine. A host missing any of them can still carry an observation — the observation is real — but a support statement derived from it is scoped to whatever was actually resolved, which in practice is usually the container kind and nothing more."
